00:20:21,600 --> 00:20:23,120
And if you bought San Francisco,

532
00:20:23,120 --> 00:20:24,000
yeah, not so much.

533
00:20:24,560 --> 00:20:26,880
Sometimes real estate buying a house

534
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:29,760
can work out in a spectacular fashion.

535
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:32,080
And that's the stories people tend to hear,

536
00:20:32,720 --> 00:20:33,520
but not always.

537
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:34,400
– And that's what I tend to see

538
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:35,360
in the comments section

539
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:36,640
when we talk about this issue

540
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:37,520
of buying a house.

541
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:39,280
I was just looking at the comments section, actually,

542
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:41,200
and on a previous conversation

543
00:20:41,200 --> 00:20:42,320
where we talked about

544
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:43,120
whether you should buy a house,

545
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:43,680
someone said,

546
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:45,200
I bought a house,

547
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:46,320
and it's the best thing I ever did.

548
00:20:46,320 --> 00:20:46,640
– Right.

549
00:20:46,640 --> 00:20:49,520
– It's launched my mindset in new directions.

550
00:20:49,520 --> 00:20:51,760
Remember that having your own space

551
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:54,160
has profound psychological impacts

552
00:20:54,160 --> 00:20:55,280
and can be life-changing

553
00:20:55,280 --> 00:20:57,360
for some that don't live

554
00:20:57,360 --> 00:20:58,800
in a healthy environment.

555
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:01,760
The psychological impact of buying a house.

556
00:21:01,760 --> 00:21:03,840
– What that commenter just said

557
00:21:05,040 --> 00:21:06,240
can be, and for him,

558
00:21:06,960 --> 00:21:08,640
obviously, it's absolutely true.

559
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,360
I am not anti-house.

560
00:21:11,360 --> 00:21:12,720
As I mentioned a moment ago,

561
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:14,960
I've owned houses most of my adult life.

562
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:17,280
But I've never bought them

563
00:21:17,280 --> 00:21:18,880
because I thought they were an investment.

564
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:20,640
I bought them because I thought

565
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:22,080
they would enhance my life

566
00:21:22,080 --> 00:21:24,320
in a way I wanted it enhanced.

567
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:26,400
They would make my life better.

568
00:21:26,400 --> 00:21:28,000
They are, in my view,

569
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:29,600
an expensive indulgence.

570
00:21:30,320 --> 00:21:32,960
I have nothing against expensive indulgences.

571
00:21:32,960 --> 00:21:34,080
That's one of the reasons

572
00:21:34,080 --> 00:21:36,320
we accumulate money, right?

573
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:38,400
I like some expensive,

574
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:39,280
some I don't care about,

575
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:39,920
some I like.

576
00:21:41,120 --> 00:21:42,240
But that's what they are.

577
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:44,240
And if you can easily afford it,

578
00:21:44,320 --> 00:21:45,520
then by all means,

579
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:47,360
buy the house.

580
00:21:47,360 --> 00:21:48,160
– Looking at some stats here,

581
00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:49,280
it says home buying was once

582
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:50,080
a solid investment

583
00:21:50,080 --> 00:21:51,360
due to rising property values

584
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:52,640
and lower mortgage rates.

585
00:21:52,640 --> 00:21:54,400
However, for younger generations,

586
00:21:54,400 --> 00:21:56,160
this is no longer the case

587
00:21:56,160 --> 00:21:57,760
because of skyrocketing home prices.

588
00:21:57,760 --> 00:21:59,120
Since 1980,

589
00:21:59,120 --> 00:22:00,640
U.S. home prices have increased

590
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:03,680
by over 300% outpacing inflation

591
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:05,120
and wage growth.

592
00:22:05,120 --> 00:22:06,880
In 2023, mortgage rates

593
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:08,160
surged past 7%,

594
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:09,120
making monthly payments

595
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:10,640
significantly higher than before.

596
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:12,880
And medium wages have only risen

597
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:16,800
by about 15% since the year 2000,

598
00:22:16,800 --> 00:22:19,360
while home prices have more than doubled,

599
00:22:19,360 --> 00:22:21,760
making home ownership less affordable.

600
00:22:21,760 --> 00:22:23,200
And lastly, the cost of renting

601
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:24,400
is often cheaper than buying,

602
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:25,200
especially in cities

603
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:27,120
where prices have outpaced wage growth,

604
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:28,480
leading many younger people

605
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:30,640
to choose renting for flexibility.

606
00:22:30,640 --> 00:22:32,080
This point of flexibility as well

607
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:33,040
is what we don't talk about,

608
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:34,800
which is the ability

609
00:22:34,800 --> 00:22:35,840
to go do something else

610
00:22:35,840 --> 00:22:36,400
in another country.

611
00:22:36,400 --> 00:22:37,280
– Exactly.

612
00:22:37,280 --> 00:22:38,400
– And my brother said this to me

613
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:39,200
when I was 20.

614
00:22:39,200 --> 00:22:40,000
My brother's very smart.

615
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:40,720
He's a year older than me.

616
00:22:41,680 --> 00:22:42,800
He's a financial genius

617
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:45,520
and has a much different brain to mine.

618
00:22:45,520 --> 00:22:46,800
And I remember when I was 20,

619
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:48,960
maybe 24,

620
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:49,600
and I was talking about,

621
00:22:49,600 --> 00:22:50,560
do I buy a house?

622
00:22:50,560 --> 00:22:52,080
And he both told me

623
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:53,440
it was the worst investment

624
00:22:53,440 --> 00:22:54,160
I could ever make.

625
00:22:54,160 --> 00:22:55,040
But he also told me

626
00:22:55,040 --> 00:22:56,640
to think about flexibility

627
00:22:56,640 --> 00:22:58,000
and my ability to get up and move.

628
00:22:58,000 --> 00:22:58,800
– Yes.

629
00:22:58,800 --> 00:22:59,600
– And I was, what do you mean?

630
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:00,560
And he said, well, listen,

631
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:02,800
you're in a certain era of your career

632
00:23:02,800 --> 00:23:07,280
where you might be called

633
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:08,720
by someone in San Francisco

634
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:10,240
who offers you a great opportunity

635
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:12,160
and you might want to go next week.

636
00:23:12,160 --> 00:23:13,440
And actually, when I look at

637
00:23:13,440 --> 00:23:14,640
how my career transpired,

638
00:23:14,640 --> 00:23:16,320
that's exactly what happened.

639
00:23:16,320 --> 00:23:17,680
I was in Plymouth

640
00:23:17,680 --> 00:23:19,280
and then I went to Manchester

641
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:19,840
for business.

642
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:21,200
Then I went to London for business.

643
00:23:21,200 --> 00:23:22,640
Then I went around the world

644
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:23,760
to San Francisco,

645
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:25,200
to New York for business.

646
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:27,200
And I'm moving with the opportunity.

647
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:29,520
And if I was anchored somewhere,

648
00:23:29,520 --> 00:23:30,880
because a mortgage does...

649
00:23:30,880 --> 00:23:31,840
– You'd be dragging that along.

650
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:32,160
– Yeah.

651
00:23:32,160 --> 00:23:33,280
And a mortgage does

652
00:23:33,280 --> 00:23:34,800
psychologically anchor you.

653
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:36,240
This is what people don't talk about.

654
00:23:36,240 --> 00:23:37,760
It creates a huge amount of guilt

655
00:23:38,320 --> 00:23:38,960
if you then

656
00:23:39,680 --> 00:23:40,800
want to get up and go.

657
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:41,840
Because in your head,

658
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:42,160
you're going,

659
00:23:42,160 --> 00:23:44,080
well, I'm going to be paying double.

660
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:47,920
– Well, I agree with everything you said.

661
00:23:47,920 --> 00:23:49,520
I agree with your brother.

662
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:50,880
Flexibility,

663
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:52,640
especially when you're young

664
00:23:52,640 --> 00:23:55,440
and your career is in a dynamic phase,

665
00:23:56,320 --> 00:23:58,320
it is not to be underrated.

666
00:23:58,320 --> 00:23:59,280
For my daughter,

667
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:01,120
I mean, she loves living in Savannah.

668
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:02,720
They've been there for three years.

669
00:24:03,360 --> 00:24:06,320
But she has an adventuresome soul.

670
00:24:06,320 --> 00:24:08,240
And, you know, she says,

671
00:24:08,320 --> 00:24:08,720
I don't know.

672
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:10,320
I mean, maybe at some point,

673
00:24:10,320 --> 00:24:11,920
I'll want to go live in Europe

674
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:12,640
or somewhere else.

675
00:24:13,200 --> 00:24:14,560
Well, if you have a house,

676
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:16,400
that complicates that decision.

677
00:24:17,040 --> 00:24:19,440
And even if you are fortunate enough

678
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:21,360
to buy in a market

679
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:22,960
where your values are rising,

680
00:24:23,760 --> 00:24:25,760
the cost associated

681
00:24:25,760 --> 00:24:27,600
with buying and selling houses

682
00:24:27,600 --> 00:24:28,560
are enormous.

683
00:24:29,280 --> 00:24:29,920
The, you know,

684
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:31,200
the real estate commission

685
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:32,880
and the taxes and what have you.

686
00:24:32,880 --> 00:24:34,640
So getting in and out of a house

687
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:37,440
is an expensive proposition.

688
00:24:37,440 --> 00:24:38,560
Getting in and out of an apartment

689
00:24:38,560 --> 00:24:39,440
doesn't cost anything.

690
00:24:39,440 --> 00:24:41,360
I mean, maybe your security deposit, right?

691
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:43,360
But that's it.

692
00:24:43,360 --> 00:24:45,280
That's very clean and simple.

693
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:48,800
But if you were to buy a house in Savannah

694
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:49,920
and then to say, you know,

695
00:24:49,920 --> 00:24:52,800
I think I want to go live in Portugal.

696
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:56,320
Well, now you've got to sell that house

697
00:24:56,320 --> 00:24:58,000
or maybe you have to run it.

698
00:24:58,000 --> 00:24:59,200
Now you're a landlord.

699
00:24:59,200 --> 00:25:00,880
You're an accidental landlord,

700
00:25:00,880 --> 00:25:02,800
which was subject to my second book.

701
00:25:03,680 --> 00:25:05,920
You know, that's not optimal.

702
00:25:05,920 --> 00:25:06,720
I mean, if you set out

703
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:08,080
to be a landlord, great.

704
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:10,240
But if you become an accidental landlord

705
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:12,800
because you can't sell your house

706
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:14,640
that you don't want to live in anymore,

707
00:25:14,640 --> 00:25:16,080
that's not so great.

708
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:19,920
So flexibility is enormously important.

709
00:25:19,920 --> 00:25:21,360
If I were to ask you,

710
00:25:21,360 --> 00:25:24,960
what is the simple path to wealth?

711
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:26,880
And you had to respond in a sentence.

712
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:29,600
What would that sentence be?

713
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:30,400
Avoid debt,

714
00:25:30,400 --> 00:25:31,760
live on less than you earn,

715
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:33,040
invest the surplus.

716
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:34,320
So let's talk about debt then.

717
00:25:35,200 --> 00:25:36,400
Why did you say avoid debt?

718
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:39,440
You can never be financially independent

719
00:25:39,440 --> 00:25:40,560
if you're carrying around debt.

720
00:25:41,920 --> 00:25:44,560
It's a ball and chain that you drag along,

721
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:45,920
especially consumer debt.

722
00:25:45,920 --> 00:25:49,120
Now, to be clear, if you're in business

723
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:52,400
and your business is carrying debt

724
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:56,800
as a function of running the operation

725
00:25:56,800 --> 00:25:57,840
for one reason or another,

726
00:25:57,840 --> 00:26:00,000
that's kind of a different thing.

727
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:01,840
But in terms of personal debt,

728
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:05,040
if you're running up credit card debt,

729
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:07,920
if you're leasing expensive cars

730
00:26:07,920 --> 00:26:10,160
or borrowing money to buy expensive cars

731
00:26:10,160 --> 00:26:10,960
or what have you,

732
00:26:10,960 --> 00:26:12,240
possibly a mortgage

733
00:26:12,960 --> 00:26:14,720
is in a slightly different category

734
00:26:14,720 --> 00:26:16,560
but it has all the disadvantages

735
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:17,520
we just talked about.

736
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:19,520
Debt's a ball and chain.

737
00:26:19,520 --> 00:26:24,480
It's like asking a swimmer to compete

738
00:26:24,480 --> 00:26:26,960
and strap in a weight around their waist.

739
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:29,360
Is it possible?

740
00:26:29,360 --> 00:26:30,240
Sure, I guess it is,

741
00:26:30,240 --> 00:26:32,480
but it's a whole lot more difficult.

742
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:34,400
So job one if you have debt

743
00:26:34,400 --> 00:26:35,280
is to blow it out.

744
00:26:35,840 --> 00:26:38,480
And I mean, blowing it out is a dream for many,

745
00:26:38,480 --> 00:26:41,280
but it's easier said than done, I guess.

746
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:42,400
It simply means that

747
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:44,800
you have to organize your life

748
00:26:44,800 --> 00:26:45,760
in such a fashion

749
00:26:46,640 --> 00:26:49,600
that you can divert some money

750
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:52,480
to either buying your freedom investments

751
00:26:52,480 --> 00:26:55,280
or if you have debt, paying off that debt.

752
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:58,160
You just have to do that.

753
00:26:58,160 --> 00:27:00,240
And people say, well, I can't do that.

754
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:01,680
You know, I need to have this.

755
00:27:02,640 --> 00:27:04,400
You know, I need to have these,

756
00:27:04,400 --> 00:27:06,000
the two least luxury cars

757
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:07,840
and we need to live in this neighborhood

758
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:09,760
and we need to send the kids to these schools.

759
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:10,720
We need to,

760
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:12,880
and I call that the tyranny of the must haves.

761
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:16,000
The more must haves you have in your life,

762
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:17,360
the less likely you are

763
00:27:17,360 --> 00:27:19,280
to become financially independent.

764
00:27:19,280 --> 00:27:21,280
Now that's your choice.

765
00:27:21,280 --> 00:27:23,120
That's an individual's choice.

766
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:25,360
It may very well be that those things

767
00:27:26,320 --> 00:27:28,000
are more important to you

768
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:29,360
than buying your freedom.

769
00:27:29,920 --> 00:27:30,800
And it's your money.

770
00:27:30,800 --> 00:27:32,640
It's not for me to tell anybody

771
00:27:33,360 --> 00:27:34,560
why they should spend their money

772
00:27:34,560 --> 00:27:36,000
or what's important to me

773
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:37,600
or what's important to them.

774
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:38,160
For me,

775
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:41,040
there was nothing I could spend my money on

776
00:27:41,040 --> 00:27:42,960
that was more important than my freedom,

777
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:44,640
which is why from the beginning

778
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:46,960
I diverted half of my income

779
00:27:46,960 --> 00:27:48,320
to buying that thing.

780
00:27:48,320 --> 00:27:49,840
It was never deprivation.

781
00:27:50,880 --> 00:27:51,520
Most people say,

782
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:53,520
oh, this is a path of deprivation.

783
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:54,720
I can't spend my money.

784
00:27:55,760 --> 00:27:56,720
Well, not for me.

785
00:27:57,920 --> 00:28:00,160
I spent every dime that ever came my way

786
00:28:00,960 --> 00:28:03,760
is just that I spent half of those dimes

787
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:07,120
on the thing that I wanted to own the most,

788
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:08,400
which was my freedom.

789
00:28:08,400 --> 00:28:10,960
And you own that by owning assets.

790
00:28:11,760 --> 00:28:14,480
So I wasn't depriving myself

791
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:17,120
any more than if somebody said,

792
00:28:17,120 --> 00:28:19,600
you know, I'm looking at buying

793
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:22,800
a Mercedes or a Volkswagen, right?

794
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:25,040
If I buy the Mercedes,

795
00:28:25,040 --> 00:28:26,480
I'm in this big fancy car

796
00:28:26,480 --> 00:28:27,760
and people will be impressed.

797
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:30,320
If I buy the Volkswagen,

798
00:28:30,560 --> 00:28:32,080
I'm in this more modest car,

799
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:33,840
but then I've got a whole bunch of money left over

800
00:28:33,840 --> 00:28:35,760
that I can spend on a wardrobe

801
00:28:35,760 --> 00:28:37,760
or going out to dinner

802
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:39,200
or a more expensive apartment.

803
00:28:39,200 --> 00:28:40,560
It's just a matter of choosing

804
00:28:40,560 --> 00:28:42,080
where you spend your money on, right?

805
00:28:42,720 --> 00:28:46,080
So one of the choices that I do,

806
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:47,680
I am under no illusion

807
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:50,800
that most people who read my book

808
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:52,720
will actually follow the simple path

809
00:28:53,360 --> 00:28:55,920
because I think there's just way

810
00:28:55,920 --> 00:28:57,760
too much cultural influence

811
00:28:57,760 --> 00:28:59,440
to spend your money elsewhere.

812
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,080
But at least the people who read the book

813
00:29:02,080 --> 00:29:03,920
and listen to this interview

814
00:29:03,920 --> 00:29:06,640
will be aware that there is something else

815
00:29:06,640 --> 00:29:07,840
they could buy with their money.

816
00:29:08,800 --> 00:29:11,200
And that's their personal freedom.

817
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:12,480
And you do that by assets.

818
00:29:13,200 --> 00:29:14,800
And there was nothing more important to me,

819
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:16,480
nothing I wanted more.

820
00:29:16,480 --> 00:29:19,200
So it was not deprivation at all.

821
00:29:20,480 --> 00:29:22,000
I reflect back on

822
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:24,960
where I used to be in my life.

823
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:26,880
And if I'd had this conversation then,

824
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:30,800
I really, really struggled with saving money

825
00:29:30,800 --> 00:29:33,840
because spending money was so closely linked

826
00:29:33,840 --> 00:29:36,480
to my sense of self and my self-esteem.

827
00:29:36,480 --> 00:29:38,000
A lot of people feel that way.

828
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:39,360
I've shared this story before,

829
00:29:39,360 --> 00:29:41,360
but when I was working in those call centers

830
00:29:41,360 --> 00:29:44,480
at Swinton's Car Insurance,

831
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:45,680
where I used to work,

832
00:29:45,680 --> 00:29:47,040
I would get my paycheck

833
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:48,080
and it might be, I don't know,

834
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:50,400
1,500 pounds or 2,000 pounds, whatever.

835
00:29:51,040 --> 00:29:52,720
And on my way home on payday,

836
00:29:52,720 --> 00:29:54,160
I'd go buy a 16 inch TV

837
00:29:55,200 --> 00:29:56,640
and I'd put it in the house.

838
00:29:56,640 --> 00:29:58,080
And then I'd try and see if I had enough money

839
00:29:58,080 --> 00:29:58,960
to buy a PlayStation.

